<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Liberia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Liberia College</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Liberia, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, is the country's main public university. It was chartered in 1851 as Liberia College, opened in 1862, and became a university in 1951. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-wilmot-blyden">Edward Wilmot Blyden</a> taught there and served as its president, and many of Liberia's leaders studied there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-liberia.jpg" alt="University of Liberia" width="225" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: University of Liberia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UL_LOGO.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Liberia declared independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a> in 1847, and four years later its legislature authorised a state college. Almost all of its money in the 19th century came from two American bodies, the New York Colonization Society and the Trustees of Donations for Education in Liberia, which also hired the teachers. Its backers were partly moved by concern that Liberians studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> might turn away from republican government. Clay-Ashland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a> competed to host the school, which delayed it until the cornerstone was laid in Monrovia on 25 January 1858.</p><p>Classes began in 1863 with seven students, and a preparatory division soon added 18 more. The country's first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-jenkins-roberts">Joseph Jenkins Roberts</a>, headed the college from 1862 to 1876, and its curriculum followed that of American colleges, with rhetoric and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Only ten students graduated between 1866 and 1902. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-wilmot-blyden">Edward Wilmot Blyden</a>, president from 1881 to 1884, women were admitted to the preparatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, and class and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> conflicts closed the college several times in the 1890s. A School of Forestry was founded in 1942 by Stephen A. Tolbert, and in 1951 the president J. Max Bond Sr. and the dean Anna E. Cooper turned the college into a university, the year the Louis Arthur Grimes law school was established. A medical school followed in 1968.</p><p>Civil strife closed the university in 1979, 1984 and 1990. On 22 August 1984 President Samuel K. Doe sent the army against students and staff protesting the arrest of two faculty members, leaving several dead and more than a hundred injured. No degrees were granted from 1989 to 1996 during the First Liberian Civil War, and the university reopened in 1997 with 6,000 students.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The university has four campuses: Capitol Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, the Fendall campus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> outside the city, the medical school campus in Congo Town and the Straz-Sinje campus in Grand Cape Mount County. An expansion of US$21.5 million at Fendall, funded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, began in April 2008. In 2019 it enrolled 18,753 students. Its alumni include the former presidents Arthur Barclay and Joseph James Cheeseman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Liberia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its early statesmen and scholars, especially Edward Wilmot Blyden, who taught at <b>Liberia College</b> and wrote on African identity, helped shape Pan-Africanism, and Marcus Garvey’s movement looked to Liberia in the early 1920s as a possible homeland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
